1. Overview

   This module lets a P-CSCF authorise media resources in a 5G core
   directly over the N5 reference point, acting as an Application Function
   (AF) towards the PCF with the Npcf_PolicyAuthorization service of 3GPP
   TS 29.514, instead of using Rx (Diameter) towards a PCRF as the ims_qos
   module does. The mapping of SDP to media components follows TS 29.513.

   N5_create() is called in the reply route of an INVITE, UPDATE or PRACK
   that completes an SDP offer/answer. The first call for a dialog and
   direction creates an Individual Application Session Context (HTTP POST
   to /npcf-policyauthorization/v1/app-sessions), containing the UE's IPv4
   address, the AF application identifier, the media components and the
   event subscription; later calls update it (PATCH). When the dialog ends
   (BYE, failure or expiry), the application session is deleted (POST
   .../delete). An answer of 404 to the delete is accepted, as the PCF may
   already have removed it.

   The PCF reports events with POST {notifUri}/notify and requests the
   termination of an application session, e.g. when the PDU session is
   released, with POST {notifUri}/terminate (TS 29.514 clauses 4.2.5.2 and
   4.2.5.3). These callbacks are received with the nghttp2 module and
   passed to N5_notification(); a termination releases the dialog, as
   ims_qos does on an Rx Abort-Session-Request. The notifUri given to the
   PCF identifies the dialog and direction, so callbacks need no other
   state.

   The callback path identifies a dialog by its hash entry and id, which
   are predictable: the nghttp2 listener must be reachable only from the
   5G core's service-based interface network (bind it with the nghttp2
   listen_addr parameter and filter it), as Diameter peers are restricted
   for Rx.

   Requests to the PCF use HTTP/2 with prior knowledge (cleartext h2c) by
   default. A request that fails on a reused connection without any
   response, e.g. because the producer closed the idle connection with
   GOAWAY, is retried once on a new connection (RFC 9113 clause 6.8).

   If the 5G core enforces OAuth2 (3GPP TS 29.500 clause 6.7), set oauth:
   the module then registers the P-CSCF with the NRF as an NF instance
   (NFRegister, then heartbeats) from a dedicated process, obtains access
   tokens with AccessTokenReq (TS 29.510) and sends them as bearer tokens
   to the PCF. They are sent synchronously by the SIP worker that executes
   the reply route, bounded by http_timeout.

2. Dependencies

   2.1. Kamailio Modules
   2.2. External Libraries or Applications

2.1. Kamailio Modules

   The following modules must be loaded before this module:
     * tm.
     * ims_dialog - the dialog must be created (dialog flag set) before
       the request is relayed.
     * nghttp2 - to receive the PCF's callbacks.

2.2. External Libraries or Applications

   The following libraries or applications must be installed before
   running Kamailio with this module loaded:
     * libcurl with HTTP/2 (nghttp2) support.
     * libjansson.

3. Parameters

   3.1. pcf_uri (str)
   3.2. notif_uri (str)
   3.3. af_app_id (str)
   3.4. dnn (str)
   3.5. events (str)
   3.6. release_headers (str)
   3.7. http_timeout (int)
   3.8. http2 (int)
   3.9. audio_default_bandwidth (int)
   3.10. video_default_bandwidth (int)
   3.11. authorize_video_flow (int)
   3.12. include_rtcp_fd (int)
   3.13. oauth (int)
   3.14. nrf_uri (str)
   3.15. nf_instance_id (str)
   3.16. nf_type (str)
   3.17. nf_ipv4 (str)
   3.18. heartbeat_interval (int)

3.1. pcf_uri (str)

   Scheme, address and port of the PCF's Npcf_PolicyAuthorization service.
   Mandatory.

   Example 1.1. pcf_uri parameter usage
...
modparam("ims_qos_n5", "pcf_uri", "http://10.0.0.13:7777")
...

3.2. notif_uri (str)

   Base URI at which the PCF reaches this P-CSCF's nghttp2 listener. The
   module appends /<dialog id>-<o|t> for each application session.
   Mandatory.

   Example 1.2. notif_uri parameter usage
...
modparam("ims_qos_n5", "notif_uri", "http://10.0.0.20:8090/n5")
...

3.3. af_app_id (str)

   Value of afAppId.

   Default value is “IMS Services”.

3.4. dnn (str)

   Value of dnn, if set (e.g. “ims”).

   Default value is “” (not sent).

3.5. events (str)

   Comma-separated AfEvent values subscribed in evSubsc (TS 29.514 clause
   5.6.3.3), the N5 counterparts of the Rx Specific-Action values a P-CSCF
   requests. Empty: no subscription.

   Default value is
   “SUCCESSFUL_RESOURCES_ALLOCATION,FAILED_RESOURCES_ALLOCATION”.

   Example 1.3. events parameter usage
...
modparam("ims_qos_n5", "events", "QOS_NOTIF,ACCESS_TYPE_CHANGE,SUCCESSFUL_RESOUR
CES_ALLOCATION")
...

3.6. release_headers (str)

   Extra headers for the BYEs sent when the PCF terminates an application
   session.

   Default value is “”.

3.7. http_timeout (int)

   Timeout in milliseconds for connecting to the PCF and for each request.

   Default value is 2000.

3.8. http2 (int)

   If 1, requests use HTTP/2 with prior knowledge; if 0, libcurl's default
   (HTTP/1.1, or h2 negotiated over TLS).

   Default value is 1.

3.9. audio_default_bandwidth (int)

   Bandwidth in kbit/s for marBwUl/marBwDl of an audio stream whose SDP
   has no b=AS line.

   Default value is 64.

3.10. video_default_bandwidth (int)

   As audio_default_bandwidth, for video streams.

   Default value is 128.

3.11. authorize_video_flow (int)

   If 0, video streams are not included in the media components.

   Default value is 1.

3.12. include_rtcp_fd (int)

   If 1, each media component gets a second sub-component with the RTCP
   flows (port + 1).

   Default value is 0.

3.13. oauth (int)

   If 1, use OAuth2 towards the PCF: register with the NRF and send access
   tokens. Requires nrf_uri and nf_instance_id.

   Default value is 0 (disabled).

   Example 1.4. oauth parameter usage
...
modparam("ims_qos_n5", "oauth", 1)
...

3.14. nrf_uri (str)

   Scheme, address and port of the NRF (NFManagement and access token
   services).

   Default value is “”.

   Example 1.5. nrf_uri parameter usage
...
modparam("ims_qos_n5", "nrf_uri", "http://10.0.0.10:8000")
...

3.15. nf_instance_id (str)

   NF instance id (a UUID) under which the P-CSCF registers with the NRF
   and requests tokens.

   Default value is “”.

   Example 1.6. nf_instance_id parameter usage
...
modparam("ims_qos_n5", "nf_instance_id", "6d682299-e82e-416f-96a3-94ef589f7e03")
...

3.16. nf_type (str)

   NF type registered with the NRF and used as nfType in AccessTokenReq.

   Default value is “AF”.

3.17. nf_ipv4 (str)

   IPv4 address put in the registered NF profile (ipv4Addresses), if set.

   Default value is “”.

   Example 1.7. nf_ipv4 parameter usage
...
modparam("ims_qos_n5", "nf_ipv4", "10.0.0.20")
...

3.18. heartbeat_interval (int)

   Heartbeat interval in seconds offered at registration; the interval
   granted by the NRF is used.

   Default value is 30.

4. Functions

   4.1. N5_create(direction[, subscription_id])
   4.2. N5_notification(path, body)

4.1.  N5_create(direction[, subscription_id])

   Creates or updates the application session for the dialog of this
   reply. Use in an onreply_route of INVITE, UPDATE or PRACK.
     * direction - “orig” if the UE is the caller, “term” if it is the
       callee. It selects whose SDP holds the UE's address; requests from
       either end of the dialog are handled.
     * subscription_id (optional) - the served user: “imsi-...” is sent as
       supi; a tel or SIP URI with a number, or “msisdn-...”, as gpsi.

   Returns:
     * 1 - success (201 for a creation, 200/204 for an update).
     * -1 - nothing to authorise or a local error (not a reply to INVITE,
       UPDATE or PRACK, no SDP, no dialog).
     * -2 - rejected by the PCF (4xx other than 408 and 429, or a 201
       without Location): repeating the same request will not help.
     * -3 - transient: no answer (connection failure or timeout), 408, 429
       or 5xx. The same request may succeed later or at another PCF.

   The PCF's answer is available in $n5(key) afterwards.

   Example 1.8. N5_create usage
...
onreply_route[N5_ORIG] {
    if(status =~ "^(18[03]|200)$" && has_body("application/sdp")) {
        if(!N5_create("orig", "$fU")) {
            xlog("L_ERR", "N5 failed: rc=$rc HTTP $n5(code) $n5(cause)\n");
        }
    }
}
...

4.2.  N5_notification(path, body)

   Handles a callback from the PCF received by the nghttp2 module. Returns
   1 for a termination (the dialog is being released), 2 for an event
   notification, -2 if the dialog no longer exists and -1 if the path is
   not an N5 callback of this module.

   Example 1.9. N5_notification usage
...
loadmodule "nghttp2.so"
modparam("nghttp2", "listen_port", "8090")

event_route[nghttp2:request] {
    N5_notification("$nghttp2(path)", "$nghttp2(data)");
    if($rc > 0) {
        nghttp2_reply("204", "");
    } else {
        nghttp2_reply("404", "");
    }
}
...

5. Pseudo-Variables

   5.1. $n5(key)

5.1.  $n5(key)

   The outcome of the last N5_create or N5_notification executed by this
   process. A key without a value gives $null.
     * code - HTTP status of the PCF's answer, 0 if there was none.
     * cause - the “cause” of the ProblemDetails in an error answer (TS
       29.514 clause 5.7), e.g. REQUESTED_SERVICE_NOT_AUTHORIZED.
     * events - comma-separated events reported: from “evsNotif” in the
       PCF's answer to a creation (events already met when the app-session
       is created, e.g. ACCESS_TYPE_CHANGE) or from the body of a
       notification.
     * access_type, rat_type - from the same report, e.g. 3GPP_ACCESS and
       NR.

   Example 1.10. $n5(key) usage
...
N5_create("orig", "$fU");
switch($rc) {
    case 1:
        xlog("L_INFO", "$n5(access_type)/$n5(rat_type), events $n5(events)\n");
        break;
    case -3:
        xlog("L_WARN", "PCF unavailable ($n5(code)), retry later\n");
        break;
    case -2:
        xlog("L_ERR", "PCF rejected the session: $n5(cause)\n");
        break;
}
...
